---
// src/components/sections/Hero.astro
// Homepage hero — v9, page-specific inspiration override (CLAUDE.md
// Section 4.5). This pass swaps the four scattered
// AutomationNotificationCard instances for the in-flow HeroDashboardPreview
// bento grid, reorders/restyles the two CTAs, and updates the eyebrow
// line. It inherits v8's light iridescent background, glass-shape
// HeroScatterDecoration, and ink-on-light text flip unchanged — those are
// condensed below rather than re-narrated in full; the extensive v7→v8
// dark-to-light migration commentary and the corner-card positioning-math
// history have been removed along with the code they described, per this
// pass's own cleanup instruction (stale comments describing removed code
// don't get left behind). AutomationNotificationCard.astro itself is
// untouched on disk — just no longer imported or used here. No nav —
// Nav.astro handles that separately, this is the hero section only.
//
// BACKGROUND (updated this pass): the previous v8 gradient was a
// four-stop, multi-hue "iridescent" wash (pale blue → lavender → pink →
// teal). Per direct request to match a new reference screenshot (a
// simple, diagonal white-to-periwinkle-blue wash, no pink/purple/teal),
// this pass swaps in a three-stop, blue-only gradient:
// `linear-gradient(135deg,#FFFFFF_0%,#EEF3FC_45%,#D3E0F9_100%)` — near-
// white at the top-left corner, through a very pale blue, deepening to a
// soft periwinkle blue at the bottom-right, matching the reference's
// tonal range and diagonal direction.
// ⚠️ Flag (carried forward, still open, now with new hex values): these
// three stops (#FFFFFF/#EEF3FC/#D3E0F9) still don't exist as
// DESIGN_SYSTEM.md tokens or in `tailwind.config.mjs`, same as the
// gradient they replace — confirm whether to formally register these
// (e.g. `hero-wash-*`) rather than leaving them as an arbitrary
// `bg-[linear-gradient(...)]` value.
// ⚠️ Flag (new): `HeroScatterDecoration.astro`'s own background note
// previously said its glass-shape decoration was "confirmed matching"
// this section's old iridescent gradient. That file is untouched by this
// pass — its internal background/glass-tint values still assume the OLD
// pink/purple/teal wash and were not re-checked against this new
// blue-only one. Not edited here per this task's scope (background-color
// change only was requested), but flagging that `HeroScatterDecoration`
// may now visually mismatch this section's background and could need a
// follow-up tint adjustment.
//
// SCATTER DECORATION (carried forward from v8, unchanged this pass): the
// glass-shape HeroScatterDecoration renders as a background layer, hidden
// below `lg` (matches that component's own documented viewport floor —
// see its header comment), sitting behind the in-flow content by DOM
// order and its own internal `-z-[5]`.
//
// STACKING CONTEXT (carried forward from v8, unchanged): the <section>
// carries `isolate` so its own gradient background paints first inside
// its own stacking context, ahead of the negative-z grain (-z-[9]) and
// scatter decoration (-z-[5]) layers — required for `position: relative`
// + negative z-index children to stack locally instead of escaping to
// the nearest ancestor context.
//
// GRAIN (carried forward from v8, unchanged): opacity 0.05,
// `mix-blend-mode: overlay` — tuned for legibility against this pale
// gradient (a bolder 0.08 was only needed for the old dark wash).
//
// DASHBOARD PREVIEW SWAP-IN (new, this pass): the old corner-card wrapper
// (absolutely positioned, `hidden lg:block`, four staggered
// AutomationNotificationCard instances with per-breakpoint rotation/
// position math) is removed entirely. HeroDashboardPreview now renders
// in-flow, as a normal block sibling directly below the badge/headline/
// CTA column — not absolutely positioned, and NOT wrapped in a `hidden`
// class, so it's visible at every breakpoint per the brief (the old
// cards were `lg:`-only because there was no room for four
// 288-320px floating cards around the centered text column at narrower
// widths; the bento grid doesn't have that problem since it's in-flow
// and stacks to a single column itself below `lg`). Confirmed no
// internal changes were needed to HeroDashboardPreview.astro — its own
// root grid is already `grid-cols-1` by default and only assembles into
// the 3-column bento at `lg:grid-cols-3`, so it degrades to a plain
// vertical stack on its own at every smaller breakpoint. Wrapped in
// `max-w-5xl` (comfortably narrower than the section's own `max-w-7xl`-
// equivalent padded box) so it doesn't stretch edge-to-edge on wide
// viewports, with `mt-12 sm:mt-16 lg:mt-20` above it for breathing room
// below the CTA row — standard Tailwind scale values, no arbitrary
// spacing. Given the `reveal` class like every other in-flow content
// piece in this section, for a consistent scroll-in entrance.
//
// CTA REORDER/RESTYLE (new, this pass): per the brief, "Join the
// waitlist" is now the primary CTA and renders first (both in DOM order
// and therefore first/left in the `sm:flex-row` layout), restyled from
// the previous blue-600 fill to DESIGN_SYSTEM.md's Primary CTA — Motion
// variant (`bg-ink`/`text-paper`, hover lift) — the exact class string
// from DESIGN_SYSTEM.md Section 3, not approximated. "See how it works"
// keeps its existing Outline-style treatment (`bg-paper`/`border-rail`)
// unchanged and now renders second. blue-600 stays visible in the
// section via the badge's "50 spots left" text and chevron icon, so the
// fixed accent isn't lost just because the primary CTA fill changed.
//
// COPY (new, this pass): eyebrow line changed from "Propose. Sign. Get
// paid." to "One workflow. Less admin." per the brief. Headline, subtext,
// note line, and "See what's inside" link are unchanged — none of them
// reference the old scattered-card layout or otherwise conflict with the
// light background/new in-flow layout, so nothing else needed rewording
// (flagging that explicitly, per the brief's "flag rather than silently
// change" instruction — there's nothing further to flag here).
//
// MIN-HEIGHT / VERTICAL CENTERING (carried forward from v8, still
// applies): `min-h-[85vh] sm:min-h-[90vh] lg:min-h-screen` plus `flex
// flex-col items-center justify-center` centers the in-flow content
// column inside that reserved height. The dashboard preview is now a
// second in-flow flex child alongside the badge/headline/CTA block —
// `min-h` is a floor, not a cap, so a taller combined content stack (now
// including the bento grid) simply grows the section rather than being
// clipped; no change needed to this rule for that reason.
//
// FIXED-HEADER CLEARANCE (updated this pass): top padding increased from
// `pt-24 sm:pt-28 lg:pt-32` to `pt-36 sm:pt-44 lg:pt-52` — the badge/
// headline block was sitting too close under Nav.astro's fixed bar, so
// this adds real extra breathing room above the content column (not just
// bar-height clearance). Bottom padding (`pb-28 sm:pb-40`) unchanged.
//
// Icon imports trimmed to just `ChevronRight` (badge chevron) — `Check`,
// `FileText`, `DollarSign`, `FolderOpen` were only ever consumed by the
// four removed AutomationNotificationCard instances.

import { ChevronRight } from 'lucide-astro';
import HeroDashboardPreview from '../ui/HeroDashboardPreview.astro';
import HeroScatterDecoration from '../ui/HeroScatterDecoration.astro';
---

<section class="relative isolate overflow-hidden bg-[linear-gradient(135deg,#FFFFFF_0%,#EEF3FC_45%,#D3E0F9_100%)] flex flex-col items-center justify-center min-h-[85vh] px-4 pt-36 pb-28 sm:min-h-[90vh] sm:px-6 sm:pt-44 sm:pb-40 lg:min-h-screen lg:px-8 lg:pt-52">
  <!-- Grain/noise texture overlay — see "GRAIN" note above. Purely
       decorative, inert SVG data-URI, no network request. -z-[9] must
       stay more negative than HeroScatterDecoration's own -z-[5] for
       correct paint order inside this section's `isolate` context. -->
  <div class="hero-grain pointer-events-none absolute inset-0 -z-[9]" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <!-- Bottom fade-to-white — softens the hard cut where this section's
       periwinkle gradient met FeaturesSummary's solid white background.
       Sits above the grain (-z-[9]) but BELOW HeroScatterDecoration
       (-z-[5]) at -z-[7], so the glass shapes keep painting on top and
       stay fully visible/unfaded, same as before this change.
       Eased multi-stop gradient (not a flat linear one) — a plain
       transparent→white linear fade whitens too quickly near the top of
       this band, which was cutting hard across the scatter shapes' own
       drop shadows there. The extra stops back-load the white into the
       last ~40% of the band so the shadows sit on gently-graduated blue
       for longer, avoiding a visible seam against their shadow edge. -->
  <div class="pointer-events-none absolute inset-x-0 bottom-0 -z-[7] h-48 bg-[linear-gradient(180deg,transparent_0%,transparent_35%,rgba(255,255,255,0.35)_60%,rgba(255,255,255,0.8)_80%,#FFFFFF_100%)] sm:h-64 lg:h-72" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <!-- Glass-shape scatter decoration — see "SCATTER DECORATION" note
       above. Hidden below `lg`, matching that component's own documented
       viewport floor, so it never competes with the centered content on
       narrower viewports. -->
  <HeroScatterDecoration class="hidden lg:block" />

  <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-2xl text-center">
    <a href="/waitlist"
      class="reveal inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 rounded-full border border-rail bg-white px-4 py-1.5
             text-sm text-ink/70 shadow-sm transition-colors duration-200 ease-in-out hover:border-ink/20"
    >
      Closed beta, cohort 3 &middot; <span class="font-semibold text-blue-600">50 spots left</span>
      <ChevronRight class="w-3.5 h-3.5 text-blue-600" stroke-width="2" />
    </a>

    <p class="reveal mt-6 text-sm text-ink/50">One workflow. Less admin.</p>

    <h1 class="reveal tracking-in-contract whitespace-nowrap mt-2 font-display text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink leading-[1.1]">
      One flow. Not five apps.
    </h1>

    <p class="reveal mt-6 text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/70 leading-relaxed max-w-xl mx-auto">
      Huzlly is the lean alternative to a pile of freelance tools. Proposals, contracts, invoices,
      and projects &mdash; connected in one automatic flow, built for solo freelancers.
    </p>

    <!-- CTA order/style — see "CTA REORDER/RESTYLE" note above: primary
         (dark-filled) first, outline second. -->
    <div class="reveal mt-8 flex flex-col items-center justify-center gap-3 sm:flex-row">
      <a href="/waitlist"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-xl bg-ink px-5 py-2.5
               text-sm font-semibold text-paper shadow-sm transition-all duration-200 ease-in-out
               hover:bg-ink/90 hover:-translate-y-0.5 hover:shadow-md active:translate-y-0
               focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ink"
      >
        Join the waitlist
      </a>
      <a href="#how-it-works"
        class="inline-flex items-center justify-center gap-2 rounded-xl border border-rail bg-paper px-5 py-2.5
               text-sm font-semibold text-ink shadow-sm transition-colors duration-200 ease-in-out
               hover:bg-rail/20 hover:border-ink/30
               focus-visible:outline focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-ink"
      >
        See how it works
      </a>
    </div>

    <p class="reveal mt-4 text-xs text-ink/50">Free to join. No credit card required.</p>

    <a href="/features" class="reveal mt-8 inline-block text-xs text-ink/50 underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink/70">
      See what's inside
    </a>
  </div>

  <!-- Dashboard preview — see "DASHBOARD PREVIEW SWAP-IN" note above.
       In-flow (not absolutely positioned), visible at every breakpoint,
       capped at max-w-5xl so it doesn't stretch edge-to-edge on wide
       viewports. -->
  <div class="reveal relative mt-12 w-full max-w-5xl sm:mt-16 lg:mt-20">
    <HeroDashboardPreview />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Grain/noise texture overlay — inline SVG feTurbulence data-URI,
     tiled at 120x120px. opacity 0.05 + mix-blend-mode: overlay keeps it
     at a "felt, not seen" level rather than visible static. Purely
     decorative — see the "GRAIN" note above for context. */
  .hero-grain {
    background-image: url("data:image/svg+xml;charset=UTF-8,%3Csvg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' width='120' height='120'%3E%3Cfilter id='n'%3E%3CfeTurbulence type='fractalNoise' baseFrequency='0.85' numOctaves='2' stitchTiles='stitch'/%3E%3C/filter%3E%3Crect width='100%25' height='100%25' filter='url(%23n)'/%3E%3C/svg%3E");
    background-repeat: repeat;
    opacity: 0.05;
    mix-blend-mode: overlay;
  }

  .reveal {
    opacity: 0;
    transform: translateY(14px);
  }
  .reveal.is-visible {
    opacity: 1;
    transform: translateY(0);
    transition: opacity 0.6s ease, transform 0.6s ease;
  }

  /* Heading gets Animista's "Tracking In Contract" instead of the generic
     translateY fade above — letter-spacing starts wide and contracts to
     normal while fading in (no Z-translate/perspective needed for this
     variant, unlike the "-bck" version). Scoped to
     `.reveal.tracking-in-contract` so it overrides the plain `.reveal`
     rule above instead of running alongside it (both animate opacity —
     letting both fire would fight each other). Same `.is-visible` trigger
     as every other `.reveal` element, so it still uses the shared
     IntersectionObserver below rather than needing its own.

     Peak letter-spacing kept at Animista's stock 1em for a clearly
     visible both-sides convergence (text is centered, so the outer
     letters visibly travel in from both left and right as spacing
     shrinks) — this is safe against reflow purely because of
     `whitespace-nowrap` on the h1 itself (Tailwind utility on the
     element, not in this block): learned the hard way from the "-bck"
     variant used earlier in this section's history that at 1em this
     24-character headline needs far more width than its box, which
     forces a genuine line-wrap reflow (2 lines -> 3 lines -> 1 line) as
     the animation plays out unless wrapping itself is disabled. With
     `nowrap` in place, any leftover extra width just overflows silently
     past the box edges for the ~1s of the animation (clipped by the
     section's own `overflow-hidden`), invisible in practice since
     opacity is still ramping up from 0 during that window.

     `margin-right: -1em` paired with letter-spacing at 0% (both left
     unset at 100%, so both implicitly animate back to their normal
     underlying values): `letter-spacing` in CSS only inserts space
     *after* each character, including a trailing gap after the very last
     one with no matching gap before the first. With centered text, that
     unmatched trailing gap pushes the whole line's visual center
     rightward, so it looked anchored on the left and growing in from the
     right only, instead of converging symmetrically from both sides. The
     matching negative margin cancels exactly that trailing gap at every
     point through the animation (both animate together at the same
     rate), so the visible glyphs stay centered and both edges move
     inward evenly. */
  .reveal.tracking-in-contract.is-visible {
    animation: tracking-in-contract 1s cubic-bezier(0.215, 0.61, 0.355, 1) both;
    transition: none;
  }

  @keyframes tracking-in-contract {
    0% {
      letter-spacing: 1em;
      margin-right: -1em;
      opacity: 0;
    }
    40% {
      opacity: 0.6;
    }
    100% {
      letter-spacing: normal;
      opacity: 1;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .reveal {
      opacity: 1 !important;
      transform: none !important;
      transition: none !important;
    }
    .reveal.tracking-in-contract.is-visible {
      animation: none !important;
      letter-spacing: normal !important;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  const prefersReducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
  const revealTargets = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal');

  if (prefersReducedMotion || !('IntersectionObserver' in window)) {
    revealTargets.forEach((el) => el.classList.add('is-visible'));
  } else {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        entries.forEach((entry) => {
          if (entry.isIntersecting) {
            entry.target.classList.add('is-visible');
            observer.unobserve(entry.target);
          }
        });
      },
      { threshold: 0.15 }
    );
    revealTargets.forEach((el) => observer.observe(el));
  }
</script>
